// Test: a for-loop body that pushes a value per iteration is rejected.
//
// This file used to pin the opposite behaviour -- the body accumulated on the
// runtime stack, `depth` observed 5 after five iterations, and the static model
// silently disagreed, treating the body as net-zero. Its note asked for this to
// change "as a conscious decision, not silently" if strict loop stack-effect
// checking ever landed. It has: the body's effect has to be zero, because the
// next iteration starts where the last one ended and the trip count is a runtime
// value. Accumulating means binding to a named local.
fn main() {
	0 5 1 for i {
		i
	}
	"depth: " print depth print nl
	clear
}
